== Chapter VIII

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Containing a sharp rebuke to those who do not love God.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

#emph[Use] 1. This may serve as a sharp rebuke to those who do not have a dram of love to God in their hearts.#footnote[#emph[Use] 1. Reproof.] Are there such wretches alive? He who does not love God, #emph[aut lapis est, aut belluae] \[is either a stone or a beast\]; he is a beast with a man’s head. O wretch, do you live on God every day, and yet not love him? Suppose a man had a friend who kept him supplied with money and gave him all his allowance. Would he not be worse than a #emph[barbarian] if he did not respect and honor that friend? Such a friend is God. He gives you your breath; he gives you a livelihood. And will you not love him? You would love your prince if he saved your life, and will you not love God, who gives you your life? What magnet is as powerful to draw love as the blessed Deity? He is blind whom beauty does not attract; he is stupid who is not drawn by the cords of love. When the body is cold and has no heat in it, it is a sign of death. That man is dead who has no heat of love to God in his soul. How can he expect love from God, when he shows no love to him? Will God ever lay in his bosom such a viper, one that spits out the poison of malice and hostility against him?

This rebuke falls heavily on the atheists of this age. They are so far from loving God that they do all they can to spite him. “Like Sodom they flaunt their sin” (Isa 3:9). “They set their mouths against the heavens”#footnote[Ps 73:9.] in pride and blasphemy, and openly defy God. These are monsters in nature, devils in the shape of men. Let them read their doom in 1 Cor 16:22: “If anyone does not love the Lord Jesus Christ, let him be Anathema Maranatha”#footnote[#emph[Idem significat vox חרם apud Hebraeos.] \[The word חרם means the same among the Hebrews.\]]\; that is, let him be accursed from God until Christ’s coming to judgment#footnote[τὸ ἀνάθεμα παντελῶς τοῦ Χριστοῦ ἀποκόπτει. \[The anathema cuts a man off from Christ entirely.\] #emph[Chrysostom,] On Anathema.]. Let him be heir to a curse while he lives, and on the dreadful day of the Lord let him hear that heart-rending sentence pronounced against him: “Depart from Me, you who are cursed.”
